1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,520
A few people ask me what you're actually meant to do with Scout when your team's on defense,

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:06,800
or getting completely rolled, or you're playing on some narrow map like Dustbolt,

3
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:10,140
where there's barely anywhere to move. And they all sound like different questions,

4
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:14,820
but it's pretty much the same problem. You can't just hold W and take whatever fight you want

5
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:19,660
anymore. Because Scout feels ridiculous when your team's doing well. Everyone's distracted,

6
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:23,760
people are getting separated, you've got space to run around, and you're usually the first person

7
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:27,540
there whenever a fight opens up. But the second your team loses all that pressure,

8
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:32,180
and every doorway starts getting watched, Scout can go from feeling like he has no limitations

9
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:34,980
to feeling like one of the most useless classes in the game.

10
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:42,500
So a lot of this video is going to be about slowing down a bit, using chip damage,

11
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:47,460
sitting further back on high ground where you can actually see what's going on, and waiting for someone to overextend

12
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:50,700
instead of launching yourself into three people every time you see their backs turned.

13
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:55,400
Because even when an opening looks really good, you still have to think about whether your team is alive,

14
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:59,440
whether anyone can actually help you and whether dying for that play is even worth it,

15
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:05,440
especially when you're defending on red in payload or attack defend because you can't take your life for granted like blue team can

16
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:09,880
because those respawn times on certain maps get just ridiculous and

17
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:14,080
one bad death can take you out of the fight for what feels like forever.

18
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:18,040
And Scout can create way more for his team than the scoreboard actually shows.

19
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:23,480
You can make half the enemy team turn around, pull someone away from the objective or force their medic backwards,

20
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:27,320
but your team still has to notice and do something with it.

21
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:33,900
So we're gonna go through all of that with some pre-recorded clips that I'm gonna lightly commentate and how to stay useful when you can't hold W.

22
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:41,760
How to know when to commit and how to tell when you made a bad play versus when your team just didn't take advantage of what you created.

23
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:46,420
In downtime situations like this on defense, chip damage is sometimes all you can really do,

24
00:01:46,420 --> 00:01:51,720
but it's more important than people think. It makes people walk back, slows them down and forces them to look for heals.

25
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:58,080
and make sure not just when pestering people, but in general, make sure you're on an elevated platform when holding any position with Scout.

26
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:02,380
It's crucial that if you get the chance to not let engineers get their sentry built in the first place.

27
00:02:02,380 --> 00:02:08,240
If it's level 3, you alert your team and when your combo comes out to deal with it, help them if you can at that very moment,

28
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:13,380
but otherwise pay attention to the flanks. The engineer was on the ball here and he starts building again,

29
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:16,400
so I call it out to my soldier friend and he deals with it straight away.

30
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:22,660
The engineer is probably thinking, oh my god, I'm so unlucky. They keep walking out soon as I build my stuff.

31
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:25,500
But it isn't luck. I'm scouting out these areas.

32
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:39,340
I being an actual scout and I communicating what I say to my team At the same time I not overextending I treating these two flanks like they my post I contesting them and I cleaning up anyone who tries to walk through But I not running forward trying to take a 1v2

33
00:02:39,340 --> 00:02:45,500
I'm just taking it slow, taking my time and making sure these areas stay contested because after all we're playing defense.

34
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:50,180
We're not the ones attacking. You don't need to hold W as much as you might think just because you're playing scout.

35
00:02:59,260 --> 00:03:07,640
Right here I'm backed into a corner by a pyro and a scout.

36
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:11,480
It doesn't look like I have anywhere to go but there's this tiny slope in the ground.

37
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:15,720
I run up it, jump backwards and it gives me just enough height to double jump forward

38
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:17,080
over the pyro's head.

39
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:20,940
That tiny bit of elevation saves me from getting flamed and just happens to set me up to kill

40
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:21,940
the scout as well.

41
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:25,660
That's one of the best skills you can have a scout, not just seamlessly using anything

42
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:29,660
around you, but identifying what can be used as high ground in the first place.

43
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:33,540
It's a bit like Emmett becoming a master builder at the end of the Lego movie, except

44
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:39,420
you're looking at every box, railing or tiny slope and thinking, can I use it as high ground?

45
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:43,040
Basically any type of elevator platform will let you get extra height and reposition with

46
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:46,320
less risk.

47
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:49,860
Like the last clip, this is a defensive situation that isn't as straightforward as holding a

48
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:52,500
boxed in last with two obvious flanks.

49
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:55,620
When you're forced to fight in more open areas without much pressure from your team,

50
00:03:55,620 --> 00:03:58,1000
the scout needs to take some of that pressure off them by becoming the centre of attention

51
00:03:58,1000 --> 00:04:00,620
without just throwing his life away.

52
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:04,640
It's about occupying spaces the enemy can't ignore, forcing them to deal with you and

53
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:16,880
drawing attention away from your team while still prioritising survivability.

54
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:20,380
Once your team is no longer occupying the space you were trying to defend and the enemy

55
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:24,280
has taken full control of that area, your presence there becomes much less valuable.

56
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:27,600
If you still have space behind you to retreat into, use it.

57
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,620
There's no reason to keep you testing an area your team no longer has any presence in, especially

58
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:32,920
when you're on defense.

59
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:37,180
Your job isn't to take the space back by yourself, it's to stay alive, continue slowing them

60
00:04:37,180 --> 00:04:39,140
down and keep running time off the clock.

61
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:41,380
But you need to be alive to do this.

62
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:45,340
Where blue team is all about trying to get one big coordinated push to break through,

63
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:46,780
reddit is more about survivability.

64
00:04:50,380 --> 00:05:03,380
The previous clip was a good example of what Scout should be doing when his team doesn't have much presence in the area, but when your team is established and actually occupying the space you're defending, your role changes.

65
00:05:03,380 --> 00:05:16,519
Now your job is to prevent threats from reaching your team by pestering them or killing them like this sniper I focus in on to prevent him landing an easy headshot on my heavy or med And clean up easy picks on enemies your power class is already engaging with

66
00:05:16,519 --> 00:05:20,519
And safely apply chip damage that forces blue to retreat or delays their push.

67
00:05:20,519 --> 00:05:27,519
You're still trying to have as much impact as possible, but without over committing and throwing away the position your team has already established.

68
00:05:33,519 --> 00:05:52,199
Another question I got was how you're meant to play scout on narrow maps like Dust Bowl.

69
00:05:52,199 --> 00:05:55,499
Honestly maps like Dust Bowl and Gold Rush are where you need to take all of this advice

70
00:05:55,499 --> 00:05:56,499
the most seriously.

71
00:05:56,499 --> 00:06:02,239
They're old restrictive maps where the balance has aged pretty badly, especially for red.

72
00:06:02,239 --> 00:06:06,619
The respawn can be anywhere from 18 to 22 seconds and the points can be captured absurdly

73
00:06:06,619 --> 00:06:07,619
quickly.

74
00:06:07,619 --> 00:06:10,959
And because of that, there isn't some secret flank or defensive strategy that suddenly

75
00:06:10,959 --> 00:06:11,959
makes Scout amazing.

76
00:06:11,959 --> 00:06:16,559
You have to be extremely deliberate about where you position yourself, which fights you take,

77
00:06:16,559 --> 00:06:18,859
and whether you are actually where your team needs you.

78
00:06:18,859 --> 00:06:22,479
High ground is still important, but only if it's actually safe.

79
00:06:22,479 --> 00:06:25,839
Most of the obvious high ground on the first section of Dust Bowl leaves you exposed to

80
00:06:25,839 --> 00:06:27,339
snipers and spam.

81
00:06:27,339 --> 00:06:31,519
Sometimes you genuinely have to play around the point and be prepared to back up once

82
00:06:31,519 --> 00:06:36,079
your team loses that space. In this clip I move around and I help my team defend

83
00:06:36,079 --> 00:06:39,799
the choke but I always end up returning to the same flank besides the point. It

84
00:06:39,799 --> 00:06:43,819
gives me cover, close-range fights and an easy escape while still allowing me to

85
00:06:43,819 --> 00:06:47,179
directly defend the point. It's also a really common place for Blue to put a

86
00:06:47,179 --> 00:06:51,139
teleporter so it's naturally somewhere Scout can find a lot of value. Target

87
00:06:51,139 --> 00:06:54,859
priority is also important. In general if you see something like a direct hit

88
00:06:54,859 --> 00:06:58,159
soldier there's a good chance he's using it because he wants to destroy your team's

89
00:06:58,159 --> 00:07:03,319
Sentry Nest. Scout can choose these fights so you should use that freedom to focus the people who

90
00:07:03,319 --> 00:07:07,379
are most threatening to your team. You need to play as a team better than Blue does on old maps

91
00:07:07,379 --> 00:07:11,599
like Gold Rush and Dust Bowl. Something I like doing is briefly moving into Blue's territory

92
00:07:11,599 --> 00:07:15,619
and getting someone's attention and then immediately retreating. Impatient players

93
00:07:15,619 --> 00:07:19,959
will often overreact and chase you too far which brings them directly into your team. That's what

94
00:07:19,959 --> 00:07:24,279
happens to this pyro when he follows me up the stairs just then and my soldier friend already

95
00:07:24,279 --> 00:07:28,119
was holding that position behind me and I knew that so he deals with the pyro straight away.

96
00:07:28,159 --> 00:07:33,299
I'm not just running away from the fight, I'm dragging the pirate into a fight that's much better for my team.

97
00:07:33,299 --> 00:07:47,639
And give coldouts a scout because I already mentioned this but he coldscout for a reason and you often the first person who can see what Blue is doing So make your team aware of anything they deserve to know about It doesn need to be complicated

98
00:07:48,379 --> 00:07:54,659
You know, it's just that their combo is coming through the top left flank or a pyro is jetpacking towards you or someone is moving into your team space.

99
00:07:54,779 --> 00:07:58,679
Even basic information just gives your team time to react and capitalize.

100
00:07:58,679 --> 00:08:04,799
Now I know not everyone wants to treat casual like a scrim and constantly give out cold outs,

101
00:08:04,979 --> 00:08:08,359
but this sixes clip is the clearest way I can show you how powerful they are,

102
00:08:08,699 --> 00:08:13,219
and how powerful Scalper comes when his team can actually capitalise on the openings he creates.

103
00:08:13,959 --> 00:08:20,119
I don't create this opening by dropping the medic, I create it by forcing basically their entire team to turn around and focus on me.

104
00:08:20,419 --> 00:08:22,659
For those few seconds, none of them are looking at my team,

105
00:08:22,759 --> 00:08:26,939
which gives everyone else the opportunity to move forward and wipe out basically their entire team.

106
00:08:26,939 --> 00:08:29,759
But that's only powerful if my team knows what's happening.

107
00:08:29,759 --> 00:08:35,819
I can't expect them to capitalize on the entire enemy team looking at me unless I call it out and make them aware.

108
00:08:35,819 --> 00:08:40,859
Once I do, they know that the opening exists and can immediately take advantage of it.

109
00:08:40,859 --> 00:08:45,359
And this is what it looks like in full swing when your team capitalizes on the opening Scout creates.

110
00:08:45,359 --> 00:08:50,359
Your value isn't always the player you kill, sometimes it's how much attention you pull away from everyone else,

111
00:08:50,359 --> 00:08:53,159
and this absolutely applies to casual as well.

112
00:08:53,159 --> 00:08:56,559
People just don't communicate it nearly enough, especially a Scout.

